 '[{"text": "8 m", "feedback": "8 is the rectangle''s length, not its width — the width is 3 m less than this."}, {"text": "−8 m", "feedback": "A negative width doesn''t make physical sense for this problem, even though it satisfies the equation algebraically — reject it and use the positive solution."}, {"text": "5 m", "feedback": "Correct. Let w be the width: w(w+3) = 40 gives w²+3w−40 = 0, which factors as (w+8)(w−5) = 0; rejecting the negative solution leaves w = 5 m."}, {"text": "4 m", "feedback": "Check 4 × 7 (width times length) — it doesn''t equal the required area of 40 m²."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-reject-negative'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'applications-of-quadratics', 22, 'Advanced',
 'A ball''s height is modelled by h = -5t² + 30t + 2. What is its maximum height?', 1,
 '[{"text": "45 m", "feedback": "This is the value of -5t² alone at the vertex — the other two terms (30t and 2) still need to be added in."}, {"text": "47 m", "feedback": "Correct. The vertex occurs at t = -30÷(2×-5) = 3; substituting gives h = -5(3)²+30(3)+2 = -45+90+2 = 47 m."}, {"text": "32 m", "feedback": "This adds only two of the three terms at the vertex — recompute -5(3)²+30(3)+2 in full."}, {"text": "2 m", "feedback": "This is only the ball''s starting height (at t=0) — the maximum height occurs later, at the parabola''s vertex."}]'::jsonb,
